<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

  <!--
    CHANGE_NETWORK_STATE is what ConnectivityManager.requestNetwork() requires. It is a
    NORMAL permission (install-time, no runtime prompt), so declaring it here is the whole
    story for the peer join and for the upstream request.

    It lives in the MODULE manifest, not in app.config.ts, on purpose: `android/` is
    prebuild output and `expo prebuild` with the clean flag regenerates the app manifest,
    while a library manifest is merged by the Android manifest merger on every build. Putting
    it here means the permission cannot be lost by a regeneration.

    ALREADY DECLARED APP-SIDE (app.config.ts `android.permissions`), and still required:
      - ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE  (reading NetworkCapabilities / LinkProperties)
      - ACCESS_WIFI_STATE
      - NEARBY_WIFI_DEVICES   RUNTIME permission; this app targets SDK 36, and per
                              https://developer.android.com/develop/connectivity/wifi/wifi-permissions
                              "Apps that target Android 13 (API level 33) or higher and manage
                              Wi-Fi connections should request the NEARBY_WIFI_DEVICES runtime
                              permission ... on previous versions of Android, these apps needed
                              to declare the ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION permission instead."
                              src/services/android_permissions.ts already requests it.
      - INTERNET              (the upstream forward)

    ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION is declared below, and the reason CHANGED. This comment used to say
    it was deliberately absent, on the grounds that NEARBY_WIFI_DEVICES covers the peer join
    on Android 13+ and that a dangerous permission was not worth a runtime-request flow the
    app did not have. That is still true OF THE JOIN. It is not true of reading the name of
    the network the phone is already on, which is the SSID prefill in "Share WiFi with
    reader": WifiInfo.getSSID() returns the literal "<unknown ssid>" to any caller without
    location permission, whichever route it is reached by (WifiManager.getConnectionInfo, or
    NetworkCapabilities.getTransportInfo on API 29+), and NEARBY_WIFI_DEVICES does not unlock
    it. See modules/reader-link/.../CurrentSsid.kt for the read and its reason codes.

    THE SCOPE OF THIS IS ONE BUTTON. The app now HAS a runtime-request flow
    (src/services/android_permissions.ts, ensureFineLocationPermission) and it is invoked
    from exactly one place: the "Use current network" button inside the WiFi-share sheet.
    Nothing requests location at launch, no other feature calls it, and the SSID field stays
    editable whether the permission is granted, denied or never asked for. Declaring the
    permission is what makes that button possible; it does not make the app a location
    consumer.

    It lives HERE, in the module manifest, for the same reason CHANGE_NETWORK_STATE does:
    `android/` is prebuild output and a regeneration would drop anything that only existed
    there, while a library manifest is merged on every build.

    NEARBY_WIFI_DEVICES IS DECLARED WITHOUT `android:usesPermissionFlags="neverForLocation"`,
    and that is now load-bearing rather than incidental. That flag is a promise to the OS
    that the app does not derive physical location from Wi-Fi results; the platform honours
    it by stripping location-bearing data from what those APIs return. An app that asserts it
    while also reading the current SSID is asserting something false, and on the strict
    reading the flag is what would keep the read redacted. Checked at the time of this
    change: the flag appears in NO manifest in this repo (app.config.ts `android.permissions`
    emits a bare uses-permission, the prebuilt android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml has
    no flags on line 5, and no dependency manifest merges one in). So nothing had to be
    removed. If anyone adds it later, the SSID prefill is what breaks.
  -->
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.CHANGE_NETWORK_STATE" />
  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_FINE_LOCATION" />

</manifest>
